title: Probe corpora do not record which rows are environment-dependent, so a stable-looking verdict can be a foreign plugin's
updated_at: "2026-08-31T08:12:51Z"
---
Probe corpora record verdicts that depend on the probing machine's installed
plugin set, but nothing in the artifact says which rows those are. A reader
cannot tell an environment-dependent row from a stable one, and the writer
cannot tell either at write time.

## Why this is filed

Measured, not speculated. Re-probing the b052 corpus on a differently
configured machine flipped exactly 3 rows of 742, and all three were `Foo::*`
spellings that resolved through a namespace belonging to an installed plugin
that is no longer installed. 739 held. The b053 corpus re-probed 592 of 592
unchanged. So the effect is small, real, and invisible in the artifact.

The damage is not the flip itself. It is that a later reader cites such a row
as evidence of something it never showed: lane-b057's B070 comment cited those
`Foo::*` rows as workspace-declared witnesses, which they are not — they were a
foreign plugin's namespace. The corpus was correct; the provenance was missing,
so the reading went wrong.

## The design question, which is the actual content of this ticket

The weak form is cheap and nearly worthless on its own: one header line naming
the probing machine's plugin set at generation time, about 5 lines in each
writer.

The useful form is marking WHICH ROWS are environment-dependent, and the writer
**cannot compute that**. `Foo::VirtualKey` and `UI::VirtualKey` are
indistinguishable to it — both are namespace-qualified names that resolved.
Only re-probing on a differently configured machine separates them, which is
how the three flips were found, by hand.

So the knowledge has to live somewhere, and today it lives in
`PLUGIN_NAMESPACE_ARTEFACTS` — a Rust test constant in
`src/typecheck/resolver.rs:303`. That is the wrong home for a fact about a data
file: the corpus can be regenerated on another machine without the constant
changing, and nothing connects them.

Decide where it belongs before writing code. Candidates worth weighing:

- a per-row flag the writer sets from a declared environment manifest;
- a separate provenance sidecar keyed by row, so the corpus stays a pure
  measurement;
- the re-probe driver (`--reprobe`) emitting the marking as a diff between two
  machines, making environment-dependence a MEASURED property rather than a
  declared one.

The third is the only one that cannot go stale, because it derives the fact the
same way the fact was actually discovered. It also costs a second machine.

## Related

B068 (batched probes fabricating ACCEPTED rows), B077 (stranded fixtures
contaminating later probes — the same class: the probe environment is state
nobody is tracking), `docs/probes/b071-enum-conversion.tsv`,
`scripts/probe_enum_corpus.py`.

## MEASURED GROUND TRUTH (lane-resolver, 2026-08-31)

### The premise is TRUE but INCOMPLETE, and the gap lands on the rows that matter

"Provenance is recoverable from data the corpus already collects" holds as far
as it goes: b052 has **63 context pins — 44 SCOPE, 20 ABSENT**, message forms
unambiguous, no third form.

**But only FOUR of the six prefixes behind the twelve
`PLUGIN_NAMESPACE_ARTEFACTS` spellings are pinned** (`Foo`, `Foo::Bar`,
`MathX`, `GameData`). **`MLHook` and `VehicleState` have NO pin row at all.**
A consumer deriving provenance from pins alone would silently classify
`MLHook::VirtualKey` and `VehicleState::VirtualKey` as engine-stable — two of
the twelve.

Overall: **234 distinct prefixes appear in ACCEPTED rows; 63 are pinned.**

**Therefore UNPINNED must be a FIRST-CLASS VALUE, never an absence defaulting
to ENGINE.** A format where the unmeasured case is spelled the same as the
measured-safe case is how the drift below happened in the first place.

Consistency check that DID hold: **zero ACCEPTED rows have a prefix pinned
ABSENT.** Keep this as a generator assertion, not a one-off check.

### Installed-plugin scan — MEASURED, not reasoned

Scanned all 44 installed plugin entries plus `.op` archives for both
`namespace X` AND (per B067) type declarations of X:

| prefix | declared by | reading |
|---|---|---|
| `MathX` | 5 installed plugins (Editor, ghosts-pp, ghosts-pp.op, map-together, test-draw-dev) | HOST |
| `MLFeed` | MLFeedRaceData.op | HOST |
| `MLHook` | MLHook.op | HOST |
| `Foo` | **nothing** | consistent with today's flip |
| `GameData` | nothing | ENGINE |
| `Discord` | nothing — and it is a **Core namespace** | ENGINE |
| `GmFunc` | nothing — `GmFunc::ERotationOrder` is a literal Nadeo dump key | ENGINE |
| `VehicleState` | **nothing declares it, but two installed plugins declare `dependencies = ["VehicleState"]`** | **LOOSE END** |

`VehicleState` is the one row that would otherwise record an INFERENCE. A
dependency can be satisfied by something that declares the namespace, by
something that does not, or by a library resolved outside the scanned paths.
**Coordinator ruling: record it UNPINNED until a probe settles it.** An
inference committed into the artefact whose purpose is separating measurement
from reasoning would defeat the ticket.

## `PLUGIN_NAMESPACE_ARTEFACTS` HAS ALREADY DRIFTED — the ticket predicted this

Two label errors, each contradicted by prose in the same repo. **No behavioural
effect** — all thirteen spellings expect the same verdict either way — so
relabelling changes NO test expectation.

**(a)** The list names `GameData::ESceneCharVisNotice`,
`GameData::ESceneVehicleVisEvent` and `GameData::VirtualKey` as plugin
artefacts, while the `UNEXPLAINED_ENGINE_SCOPES` doc comment **~50 lines below
in the same file** says GameData is NOT a plugin artefact and "was mislabelled
as one when first written" — and keeps a fourth GameData row in that other
list.

**(b)** Its doc comment still says `Foo` compiled "because the probe plugin
declared it itself", which `global_scope.rs:527` explicitly corrects: the probe
emits no `namespace` in either template, so `Foo` was a foreign plugin's.

When a file's own doc comment 50 lines down says a list entry was mislabelled
while the list still carries it, the file is already telling you which one is
wrong. A hand-maintained list drifted from the fact inside this repo's own
history — which is the argument for deriving it rather than maintaining it.

## DESIGN + COORDINATOR RULING

Sidecar `docs/probes/b052-scope-provenance.tsv`, keyed by scope prefix:
prefix / pin / origin / evidence. `pin` recomputed mechanically from the
corpus's own pin-row MESSAGE FORMS. `origin` one of
ENGINE / HOST / ENGINE_UNMODELLED / UNKNOWN, recorded **with evidence**, since
that is the half a compile probe cannot compute. Offline `--scope-provenance`
generator carries `origin` forward by prefix and writes UNKNOWN for any unseen
prefix, so a regenerated corpus surfaces a new scope loudly. Both hardcoded
lists in `resolver.rs` are deleted in favour of deriving from the sidecar.

**The generator must REFUSE to emit ENGINE_UNMODELLED without an evidence
cell.** "The engine has it, our typedb does not" is a claim, not a default;
UNKNOWN is the honest default.

**MUST SHIP AS TWO COMMITS** (coordinator ruling):

1. **Mechanism, provably inert.** Derive the set from the sidecar carrying
   TODAY's labels, mislabelled GameData rows included exactly as they are, plus
   a test asserting the derived set is byte-identical to the current hardcoded
   set. That test IS the deliverable. If the sets are not identical, STOP and
   report — that is a finding, not a conflict to smooth over. Both guards land
   here, protecting the mechanism before any label moves: a HOST prefix marked
   ENGINE reddens the corpus sweep; an ENGINE prefix marked HOST contradicts the
   shipped typedb.
2. **Data, diff visible.** Move the four GameData rows to the ENGINE side in
   its own commit, showing the explicit set difference and stating that no test
   expectation changed.

Rationale: this batch reverted a CORRECT merge because a bisect changed two
variables at once. A refactor and a data change landing together means a later
surprise cannot be attributed to either half. Separated, each is falsifiable
alone.